# App-Specific Design Identity

## Objective

Use the existing design system as a reusable foundation while giving every new application its own distinctive, cohesive visual identity.

## Before Implementing a New App

1. Analyze the application's purpose, target audience, functionality, and brand personality.
2. Review the existing skeleton and identify which design tokens, components, and layout patterns can be customized.
3. Establish an app-specific design direction before implementing pages. Define the color palette, typography, visual hierarchy, component styling, layout approach, surface treatments, and overall visual personality.
4. Ensure the new design is meaningfully different from the default skeleton and previous app designs. Do not simply change the primary color.

## Design System Rules

* Preserve the reusable foundation, component architecture, semantic token names, spacing scale, responsive behavior, accessibility standards, and shared functionality.
* Customize app-specific design tokens and approved component variants instead of modifying shared foundations unnecessarily.
* Keep all pages within the same application consistent with its established design direction.
* Use centralized semantic tokens rather than scattered hardcoded colors, spacing values, typography, and decorative styles.
* Establish clear visual hierarchy, readable typography, sufficient contrast, and intentional use of color.
* Avoid washed-out interfaces, low-contrast text, excessive muted colors, inconsistent spacing, arbitrary border radii, and unnecessary decoration.
* Do not introduce new visual styles on individual pages without a design-system reason.
* Do not force the same card, navigation, layout, or surface treatment onto every application when a different treatment better suits the product.
* Reuse existing components where appropriate. Extend them through consistent variants when the product requires a different appearance.
* Keep the app-specific theme and customization isolated so that changes do not unintentionally affect other projects using the skeleton.

## Decision Rule

Consistency within the current application is mandatory. Visual similarity to other applications built from the skeleton is not.

Before implementation, copy `docs/DESIGN_DIRECTION.template.md` to `DESIGN_DIRECTION.md` and fill it in. The permanent foundation lives in `docs/DESIGN_SYSTEM.md`. Then update the app-specific tokens in `src/config/brand.config.ts` and use that direction consistently.

New pages use the shared components in `src/components/ui` (`Button`, `Input`, `Select`, `Checkbox`, `FormField`, `Card`, `Badge`, `Alert`, `EmptyState`, `Modal`, `Tabs`, `Table`, `Skeleton`, `PageHeader`, `PageContainer`, `SectionHeader`) and semantic theme tokens. Do not invent a new button, card, or field treatment on a single page.

Do not launch a local browser or perform browser-based validation unless explicitly requested.
